"""Manually selected and formulated problems; claim records supply exact locators and checked references."""
from pathlib import Path
import json
R=Path('/workspace/scratch/a77a578fa7d4/SA12_work/reviews/31')
C={x['id']:x for x in json.loads((R/'claims.json').read_text())}; P=[]
def p(n,title,claims,his,today,worth,why='The supplied pages leave this step open or compressed. No opened historical source explains a decision to abandon it; no motive is inferred.'):
 cs=[C['CL-SA12-'+str(i)] for i in claims]
 P.append(dict(id='MP-SA12-'+str(n),title=title,occurrences=[dict(cote='31',scans=c['scans'],lines=c['lines'],claim=c['id']) for c in cs],as_he_states=his,plausible_today=today,state_of_art=' '.join(c['id']+': '+c['fate'] for c in cs),why_open=why,worth=worth,refs=list(dict.fromkeys(k for c in cs for k in c['refs']))))
p(101,'Finite flat sheaves versus finite group coefficients',[401,404],'Compare the two dimensions defined using all p-primary abelian sheaves and finite representable commutative p-groups on the finite-flat site; the torus criterion is part of this programme.','Fix the small finite-flat site, the coefficient category, and its comparison with fppf before any equality is asserted. Shatz’s unbounded all-sheaf examples over imperfect fields rule out identifying categories indiscriminately.','Worth a focused clarification: it distinguishes two genuinely different quantifiers before further computation.')
p(102,'Replacement of the torus divisibility argument',[405],'The first proof asks to verify a map between p-divisible groups; it is cancelled and replaced by a prime-to-p extension argument.','Use restriction–corestriction after passing to the p-Sylow fixed field, then a trivial-factor filtration. No surjectivity follows merely because both groups are p-divisible.','Worth documenting as an internal proof correction, with little new research needed.','The author’s cancellation and replacement are visible at scans7 and9; the page itself gives the reason to stop using the first route.')
p(103,'Countable finite-clopen isotriviality criterion',[413,416],'Relate isotriviality to a countable increasing union of finite clopen subgroups in the character scheme; the converse proof is compressed.','Retain finite generation and base hypotheses of SGA3X5.11bis; distinguish the auxiliary countable constant group from the character module of the group being split.','Worth one exact proof audit because the later editor also flags the converse.')
p(104,'A quotient of an old-style cofinite module',[418,419,420,421],'Construct M with finite annihilator of f but nonfinite M/fM, using successively glued modules and regularity of g.','The explicit C[[x,y]] shift-module example works for the SGA2 definition of f-cofinite. It is not cofinite under the later all-Ext definition.','Worth a short standalone exposition to prevent a terminology error from becoming a false theorem.','The manuscript pursues the construction rather than abandoning it; the apparent paradox comes from a later change in the meaning of cofinite.')
p(105,'Finite transcendence degree and real embeddings',[423],'Would finite transcendence degree suffice to embed the real closed field into R?','No. A real closure of Q(t) ordered with t larger than every integer has transcendence degree1 and cannot embed into R because the order of a real closed field is determined by its squares.','Worth retaining as a small complete counterexample; no open theorem remains.')
p(106,'Regular local dimension bounds in mixed characteristic',[427,428],'Can the prime/characteristic restriction in the regular local residue-field bound be removed, and what happens with imperfect residue fields?','Separate invertible primes, the real2-primary exception, and the residue-characteristic p-rank term. Gabber–Orgogozo’s Kato p-dimension is the correct mixed-characteristic invariant in excellent henselian cases.','Worth a hypothesis-by-hypothesis comparison, especially because ordinary Galois cd_p and Kato p-dimension differ.')
p(107,'The cancelled henselian equality',[429,485,486],'The page first suggests equality for henselian local rings, then deletes henselian and the equality query.','For strictly local noetherian integral A and ℓ invertible, cdℓFracA=dimA is now established. For arbitrary henselian residue field retain the residue contribution and exact hypotheses.','Worth recording the solved strict-local case beside the original deletion.','The deletion is documentary evidence only; it does not state why the broader proposed equality was dropped.')
p(108,'Finite-range Čech comparison boundaries',[433,434],'Find equivalences between the finite-degree effacement and comparison conditions; an early equivalence is replaced and a +1 boundary is uncertain.','Use the surviving implication diagram and incoming differential bidegree(n−r,r−1); a precise final criterion needs the exact range of all refinements.','Worth a bounded proof audit after scan confirmation; the main uncertainty is a boundary and quantifier, not existence of a spectral sequence.')
p(109,'Algebraizing formal vector bundles as vector bundles',[435,436,437],'The coherent algebraization need not be locally free, hélas; purity or parafactorial fiber hypotheses should improve the conclusion.','SGA2XII5.10 gives full faithfulness and coherent algebraization under universal coherence in degrees0,1. Additional explicit local purity/parafactorial hypotheses must ensure an equivalence for the chosen vector-bundle, line-bundle, or cover category.','Worth a focused catalogue of exact sufficient local conditions; do not conflate the three categories.')
p(110,'Nonproper Picard representability',[438],'Use Murre and the formal-existence argument to represent Picard functors of some nonproper schemes and remove properness assumptions.','Specify the schemes, the relative Picard quotient, and any boundary or rigidification. There is no single theorem covering every nonproper Picard functor.','Worth formulating one concrete class first; the manuscript’s unrestricted programme is too broad for a yes/no verdict.')
p(111,'The next degree in formal comparison',[441],'The source asks about injectivity at the next degree and leaves several comparison inequalities blank.','State the t-adic completion comparison with its exact cohomological finiteness and Mittag–Leffler assumptions; the boundary must be proved through the long exact sequence and inverse limit.','Worth one finite diagram chase once the scan settles its inequalities.')
p(112,'Relative trace and stable elements',[452,453],'Under a relative trace identity, prove that restriction and corestriction are bijective onto/from stable elements.','Restriction identifies the G-cohomology with stable H-elements; ordinary corestriction is onto but need not be injective on the stable subgroup. The faithful C4 action on Z^6 gives a counterexample.','Worth extracting the corrected theorem and counterexample together; the extra bijectivity is definitively false.')
p(113,'Relative tame inertia criterion',[454],'Extend trace-one and cohomological triviality to a subgroup H using the indicated inertia containment conditions.','A sufficient and exact cohomological condition is1∈Tr_H^G(B^H). Derive it from clearly specified decomposition/inertia data rather than guessing the compressed group-containment phrase.','Worth a local trace calculation after the ink of the subgroup condition is checked.')
p(114,'Residual H¹ of units',[456,523],'Is H¹(G,O×)→H¹(Gi,κ×) bijective for a semilocal ring and inertia group Gi?','It fails for O=F2[ε]/ε³ with ε↦ε+ε². For a local Artin ring with |G| invertible, reduction H¹(G,O×)→H¹(G,κ×) is an isomorphism; passing to inertia requires the residual quotient action as well.','Worth developing a sharp corrected statement in the tame/henselian cases; the wild counterexample is already complete.')
p(115,'Residual nonabelian H¹ for GL_n',[457],'What is the analogous assertion for GL_n(O)?','The unqualified comparison is false at n=1. A refined theorem needs explicit henselian/tame hypotheses and the appropriate residual descent data for rank-n modules.','Worth a separate descent task after the unit case, because nonabelian lifting has additional structure.')
p(116,'Completion and Tate cohomology',[458],'Find noetherian hypotheses ensuring completion commutes with Tate cohomology; analyze the displayed dense-image quotient.','For noetherian adic A, finite G and finitely generated A[G]-module M, a complete resolution with finite free terms and exactness of completion give the comparison. Unqualified dense-image statements remain separate.','Worth writing the precise sufficient theorem and one failure example outside finite generation.')
p(117,'Archimedean sign factors in the cancelled idèle sequence',[459],'Does the real-place sign factor inject unchanged, or should one divide by a subgroup; is the resulting quotient the intended abelian ramification group?','Class field theory uses the quotient of the relevant idèle group by the closure of global principal elements/units, with all finite and real places specified.','Worth reconstructing the exact ray-class modulus, not treating the cancelled diagram as an established sequence.','The whole draft is cancelled, and its own questions identify the unresolved unit intersection and quotient.')
p(118,'When surjective unit norms imply no ramification',[460],'Is norm-surjectivity on units equivalent to absence of ramification, after deleting normality?','For finite abelian extensions of nonarchimedean local fields with finite residue fields, local reciprocity gives the equivalence. Over C((t)), the ramified quadratic extension has surjective unit norms, so the residue-field convention matters.','Worth a precise scope note; a new general theorem is not suggested by the page.')
p(119,'Five cohomological vanishing criteria',[461,462],'Prove the displayed equivalences involving all commutative algebraic groups, finite modules, irreducible modules, tori, and units of finite separable algebras.','Keep cohomology topology, degree threshold, perfection, connectedness and finite-type conventions explicit. Finite-module dévissage is sound; the source does not complete every implication.','Worth a single modern reconstruction with each arrow separately proved.')
p(120,'Perfect-field multiplicative–unipotent decomposition',[463],'Verify G=Gu×Gs over the perfect base field in the commutative affine case.','For commutative affine algebraic groups over a perfect field, the product structure is established; no such split product is automatically available over an imperfect field.','Worth resolving as a standard dependency with the exact scope, not a research conjecture.')
p(121,'Continuous cohomology and filtered torsion unions',[465],'Extract the lemma justifying the essential filtered limit over finite torsion subgroups.','Continuous cohomology of a profinite group with discrete modules commutes with filtered direct limits; compact cochain domains have finite image. The index system must be divisibility-directed.','Worth a short proof because it explains a repeatedly used transition.','The text explicitly requests the lemma; MilneCFTII4.4 provides the established result.')
p(122,'Vector-group classification and nonlinear descent',[466],'Use H¹(k,GL_n)=0 to descend an exponent-p commutative unipotent group to a vector group.','For smooth connected commutative unipotent groups of exponent p over a perfect field the classification holds. A bare GL_n cocycle argument needs a chosen linear structure; additive polynomial automorphisms are larger than GL_n.','Worth isolating the exact missing linearization step, without calling the true classification false.')
p(123,'The unwritten arbitrary-field continuation',[467],'Supposons que k soit quelconque, then the proof stops.','The general-field version requires inseparable descent and nonsplit unipotent groups; no perfect-field product or GL_n proof may be imported unchanged.','Worth developing only after the exact vanishing theorem and degree threshold have been fixed.')
p(124,'Norm-one regular representation and its exception',[468,524],'The regular group-algebra norm should give a nontrivial kernel and reduce a torus dimension; the exceptional case is excluded without proof.','The unique elementary-abelian exception is C2. Adding the inverse determinant character restores a faithful determinant-one representation but changes the dimension used in the induction.','Worth a short repair task to determine whether the larger torus still proves the intended cohomological implication.')
p(125,'Unspecified Tate vanishing after Tsen',[470],'The page writes hatted cohomology zero in all degrees over an uncertain extension field.','Ordinary Galois cohomology of Gm, finite-group Tate cohomology and norm-surjectivity are distinct statements. Define the extension and the hats before asserting all-degree vanishing.','Worth checking the scan and local notation first; mathematics alone cannot choose the meaning.')
p(126,'Artin–Schreier endpoint',[472],'Reste à prouver the characteristic-p case, followed by the Artin–Schreier exact sequence.','Use0→Fp→Ga→Ga→0 and additive acyclicity to obtain cd_p≤1; the needed separably closed field makes x^p−x surjective.','Worth closing the written step with this standard calculation.')
p(127,'Closed restriction of injective sheaves',[474,475,476,525],'Does an injective sheaf stay acyclic on a closed subset? The answer is marked negative, followed by progressively stronger examples.','The explicit two-curve two-intersection construction gives nonzero H¹, and a graph-surface construction gives irreducible closed Y of dimension2 inside an irreducible3-fold. The inverse-image sheaf retains ambient germs.','Worth a careful worked example because replacing inverse image by functions on Y destroys the counterexample.','The pages answer the question negatively; some earlier attempts are cancelled because nonflasqueness alone does not prove nonacyclicity.')
p(128,'Flasqueness on an irreducible curve',[526],'On an irreducible curve the induced sheaf is always flasque, facile.','Specify whether this concerns all ambient injectives or the all-functions sheaf in the example. This run does not use the compressed assertion as a proved general theorem.','Worth a small independent sheaf-theoretic check; its quantifier is the unresolved part.')
p(129,'The p-field differential invariant',[479],'Relate δ, N, p-bases, differentials and field extensions in a page whose notation is partly undefined.','For fields of characteristic p, p-rank equals the dimension of absolute differentials and finite extensions preserve p-rank. Any sharper displayed inequality must first define δ and N.','Worth recovering definitions from adjacent cotes before further proof work.')
p(130,'Support-dimension criterion and its undefined φ variant',[482,483,484],'Construct a φ-function giving cohomological-dimension bounds, then assert a field-extension inequality for φ.','SGA4X4.1 gives the exact sufficient criterion. The spectral inequality uses p+q, while an arbitrary φ does not satisfy the later extension inequality without its intended definition.','Worth a textual-to-published comparison; the criterion is solved and the extra variant is underdefined.')
p(131,'Strict-local fraction-field dimension',[485,486],'For good strictly local schemes the fraction-field inequality is probably always true; a henselian lower bound is questioned.','For every strictly henselian noetherian integral A and invertible ℓ, cdℓFracA=dimA. General residue fields and noninvertible primes require separate statements.','Worth highlighting as a fully resolved central question, not proposing it again as open.','ILO XVIII-B4.1.2 proves the general upper bound; SGA4X2.4 proves the reverse under the checked real-prime restriction.')
p(132,'Local affine Lefschetz and simultaneous induction',[489,490,491,492,495],'Theorems1_n and2_n, torsion necessity, the good-ring condition, and a last one-degree gap are left in a simultaneous induction.','For quasi-excellent strictly local bases, ILOXV gives the dimension-function theorem for torsion invertible coefficients. Arbitrary noetherian bases admit counterexamples; the finite-type-over-field/trait induction is SGA4XIV.','Worth reconstructing the dependency graph and index correction; the principal mathematical fate is established.')
p(133,'Affine and nonaffine max-dimension bounds',[493,494],'Bound cd by max(dimX0,dimX′+1) in the affine case and the doubled analogue in general.','The quasi-excellent affine dimension-function theorem is established. The exact nonaffine max formula needs its catenarity, dimension and base hypotheses stated before matching a theorem.','Worth a scoped comparison after fixing good and the meanings of X0,X′.')
p(134,'Pointwise affine dimension bound',[496],'Propose cdℓX≤sup_x(cdℓκ(x)+dimO_X,x), then reject it using an integer-ring example.','The proposed all-noetherian bound is false, but the source example violates invertibility of ℓ. Gabber’s nonexcellent strictly local examples give a valid later refutation by combining two exact published bounds.','Worth retaining both the invalid original argument and the valid modern counterexample.')
p(135,'Affine dimension versus fraction-field dimension',[500],'For good integral affine X, cdℓX≤cdℓK; equality should hold on sufficiently small opens.','Established in important finite-type settings through affine Lefschetz. Arbitrary noetherian X is false; the good-class and uniform small-open quantifier need explicit formulation.','Worth a sharply restricted follow-up, not a blanket claim that Gabber settled every version.')
p(136,'Mixed-characteristic affine bounds and the +3 term',[501],'Use max(cdℓK,δ(Vℓ)+3), try and delete +1, then retain a further assertion after inverting another prime.','Kato p-dimension and Gabber–Orgogozo settle exact excellent henselian field/generic-open cases. The +3 affine-global expression and final other-prime assertion remain unmatched.','Worth mapping the different invariants and inequalities before attempting optimization.')
p(137,'Mixed-characteristic henselian affine opens',[502],'Every affine open of a good henselian mixed-characteristic local scheme should have cd≤dimA+δ(k)+cdℓk.','Gabber–Orgogozo6.1 proves equality dimA+dim_p(k) for nonempty affine opens inside SpecA[1/p]. Opens meeting the special fiber and the comparison of dim_p(k) with δ+ordinarycd are additional questions.','Worth a focused extension/translation task because a strong exact theorem already covers the generic open.')
p(138,'The characteristic-zero Artin announcement',[504],'Everything is verified for good henselian local rings of residue characteristic zero, thanks to Artin.','Read this through exact SGA4X/XIV statements, not as a universal quantifier over all preceding variants.','Worth an editorial cross-reference, with no separate research project until everything is disambiguated.')
p(139,'Normal extension from a punctured divisor',[509],'Prove étaleness from splitting modulo t off the closed point, using formal algebraization and a chain condition; slightly weaken splitting to an extendible étale cover.','SGA4XV3.3 and3.4 give the completed theorem and technical scope; the quotient-of-regular and formal-normality alternatives should be transcribed exactly.','Worth comparing draft to proof because it exposes the exact algebraization input.')
p(140,'The tame two-dimensional Abhyankar reduction',[510,511],'Make explicit the lemma reducing a tame cover to T^n−x, and justify dropping the quotient-of-regular condition in the curve argument.','After strict henselization, regularity, a single smooth branch divisor and invertible group order give the tame Kummer form. Complete splitting along the transverse divisor forces n=1.','Worth a short proof with each reduction stated, especially the excellence/approximation step.')
p(141,'Smooth local1-asphericity',[512],'Reduce a smooth morphism to the relative curve case, then the proof stops.','SGA4XV2.1 proves local acyclicity and local1-asphericity for invertible primes, with the tame cover lemmas supplying the missing continuation.','Worth a precise published link; the theorem itself is established.')
p(142,'Nonabelian H² base change',[514],'Extend smooth-base-change and separably-closed-field invariance to noncommutative H¹ and H².','Haine–Holzschuh–Wolf2024 theorem0.1/corollary2.30 establishes prosmooth base change for truncated Σ-torsion étale sheaves of spaces on qcqs schemes; its1.9 fixes coefficients and its proof uses Giraud’s groupoid theorem. Translate the draft’s banded H² into this framework before asserting a more unrestricted comparison.','Worth a short formulation-and-reference task: the modern nonabelian base-change programme is established, while the historical coefficient conventions need identification.')
p(143,'Duality and local-constancy equivalences',[516],'Equate the three direct-image local-constancy conditions by localization and duality.','Retain smooth/proper family assumptions, invertible constructible coefficients and derived duality. Check whether the localization argument should reference(ii) rather than(i).','Worth a diagram audit; the label mismatch and missing ambient setup are concrete.')
p(144,'Normal-crossings and elementary-morphism extension',[517],'Flat Y/S with normal-crossings fibers may satisfy the same conditions; give the elementary-morphism case.','A relative strict normal-crossings pair with smooth strata is a controlled candidate. Fiberwise normal-crossings without relative compatibility does not specify a local-acyclicity theorem.','Worth testing one relative model; the manuscript programme is not a precise solved claim.')
p(145,'A sheaf defined only on the complement and wild ramification',[518],'Study Rh_*G when G lives only on V; the problem seems difficult even with smooth boundary because of wild ramification.','Separate tame extension/local-acyclicity statements from wild nearby-cycle variation. Across codimension≥2, finite local systems extend under regularity/purity hypotheses, not by codimension alone.','Worth one tame-versus-wild model calculation with explicit ramification.','The source itself names wild ramification as the difficulty; this is the one causal explanation directly supported on the leaf.')
p(146,'A single cofinal torsion family for density',[505],'The final remark proposes conditions on an integer m so that selected torsion subgroups suffice; the statement breaks off.','SGA3IX4.10 completes it: use the subgroups killed by m^r, where m is not a pure residue-characteristic power at any point and its prime divisors include those of the torsion character type.','Worth an exact editorial link to the published continuation; this question is settled in print.')
(R/'problems.json').write_text(json.dumps(P,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
print(json.dumps({'problems':len(P),'first':P[0]['id'],'last':P[-1]['id']}))
